digraph ReasoningTree {
  // paper_id=s41467-025-56609-z
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, incorporating ratiometry into gas sensors is a promising strategy to overcome accuracy loss due to temperature-driven baseline drift."];
  "N11" [label="(11,0,0) If a gas sensor is ratiometric, then it requires a suitable reference factor that neutralizes baseline drift."];
  "N12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, a proposed solution is to use a material’s photoelectric response Rph as an internal reference for its gas-sensitive response Rgas."];
  "N13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, this photoelectric-reference ratiometric strategy requires a single material that is highly active to both light (at a given wavelength) and target gas molecules."];
  "N14" [label="(14,0,0) If gas sensing materials typically need defects for activity while light-sensing materials typically minimize defects for photoelectric performance, then combining strong gas response and strong photoelectric response in one material is intrinsically difficult."];
  "N15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, a thorough understanding and design approach to harmonize gas-sensing and photoelectric functionality in one material is still out of reach."];
  "N16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattice materials are emerging as promising gas-sensing materials and may rebalance gas sensitivity vs photoelectric performance."];
  "N17" [label="(17,0,0) If a material has a distinctive multi-quantum-well superlattice structure, then quantum confinement effects can amplify its photoelectric response."];
  "N18" [label="(18,0,0) If a superlattice surface provides long-range ordered functional groups, then these groups can replace defect-based activity by providing abundant active sites and thus improve gas-sensor sensitivity."];
  "N21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, AgBDT (an organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattice) is introduced as a high-efficiency ratiometric gas-sensing material."];
  "N27" [label="(27,0,0) Currently, the ratiometric signal (ratio) is highly responsive to changes in NO2 concentration, supporting AgBDT’s NO2 detection capability."];
  "N29" [label="(29,0,0) Currently, the ratiometric gas sensing is impervious to operational temperature fluctuations and substantially improves precision (e.g., reduced CV), implying temperature-drift compensation is achieved."];
  "N30" [label="(0,0,0) If chemiresistive sensors are attractive for cost/size but suffer temperature-induced baseline drift that harms precision, then a desirable research direction is a low-cost, in-sensor/self-referenced method that neutralizes temperature-driven drift without extra circuitry/algorithms."];
  "N31" [label="(0,0,0) If ratiometry reduces common-mode errors from varying conditions, then using an internal reference signal that shares environmental sensitivities with the gas signal can suppress temperature-induced baseline drift in the ratio."];
  "N32" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently chemiresistive sensors have temperature-driven baseline drift, and if a self-referenced ratiometric design can cancel temperature effects, then baseline drift would no longer compromise precision; therefore, a ratiometric self-reference approach is a plausible way to solve the drift problem."];
  "N34" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Currently it is hard to combine defect-reliant gas sensing and defect-minimizing photoresponse in one material, and if organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattices provide quantum-well-enhanced photoresponse plus functional-group-based gas activity, then they could satisfy both requirements; therefore, superlattices are a plausible material platform for photoelectric-referenced ratiometric gas sensing."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, temperature variations cause baseline resistance R0 drift, and such drift compromises sensor precision."];
  "N8" [label="(8,0,0) If a measurement uses ratiometry (a ratio of two or more signals), then measurement precision can be enhanced."];
  "N9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, ratiometric approaches effectively reduce errors caused by varying experimental conditions."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate a photoelectric-response-referenced ratiometric NO2 gas-sensing strategy using AgBDT and related organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattice design principles, with the aim of reducing temperature-driven baseline drift while maintaining sensitive NO2 detection.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a gas-sensing material can provide a target-gas-responsive signal and an internal photoelectric reference signal, ratiometric self-referencing can be investigated as a way to reduce temperature-driven baseline drift while preserving sensitive gas detection."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) A ratiometric gas-sensing strategy can be built around using a material’s photoelectric response Rph as an internal reference for its gas-sensitive response Rgas, provided the material is active to both light and the target gas."];
  "R12" [label="(0,0,0) Long-range ordered functional groups on a superlattice surface are a plausible design route for improving gas-sensor sensitivity in organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattice materials."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) AgBDT exhibits NO2-responsive ratiometric gas-sensing behavior, as indicated by a ratiometric signal that changes with NO2 concentration."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) The AgBDT ratiometric gas sensor shows NO2 detection capability, but the specific 3.06 ppb detection limit is not established by the selected premises."];
  "R15" [label="(0,0,0) Self-referenced ratiometric gas sensing should be developed and evaluated as a strategy for compensating temperature-driven baseline drift and improving gas-sensor precision."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattices are plausible candidates for combining gas-sensing promise with enhanced photoelectric response, rather than being established as robust for both functions solely from the given premises."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Using a material’s photoelectric response Rph as an internal reference for its gas-sensitive response Rgas provides a route to suppress temperature-induced baseline drift only if that reference neutralizes the relevant baseline drift in the ratiometric signal."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) AgBDT is supported as a candidate ratiometric NO2 gas-sensing material because its ratiometric signal responds to NO2 concentration changes."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The material-design hypothesis is to investigate organic–inorganic hybrid covalent superlattices that combine quantum-well-enhanced photoresponse with functional-group-based gas activity as candidates for dual photoelectric and gas-responsive sensing."];
  "N30" -> "N32" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
